1
00:00:00,174 --> 00:00:03,355
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:04,063 --> 00:00:08,185
In today's T-Level Chemistry lesson, we are going to study A eleven

3
00:00:08,266 --> 00:00:11,204
point one, Principles of Collision Theory.

4
00:00:12,028 --> 00:00:15,453
By the end of this lesson, you should be able to explain

5
00:00:15,604 --> 00:00:19,435
why reacting particles must collide, define

6
00:00:19,505 --> 00:00:23,673
activation energy, distinguish successful and unsuccessful

7
00:00:23,743 --> 00:00:27,353
collisions, and explain why particles must also meet

8
00:00:27,562 --> 00:00:30,058
in the correct spatial orientation.

9
00:00:30,453 --> 00:00:32,752
Let us start with a simple question.

10
00:00:33,147 --> 00:00:35,584
Two reactant particles collide.

11
00:00:36,293 --> 00:00:38,789
Must a reaction always occur?

12
00:00:39,474 --> 00:00:40,693
The answer is no.

13
00:00:41,076 --> 00:00:43,096
Many collisions are unsuccessful.

14
00:00:43,526 --> 00:00:47,148
In many reactions, existing bonds have to break before

15
00:00:47,194 --> 00:00:50,283
atoms can rearrange and new bonds can form.

16
00:00:50,956 --> 00:00:54,764
That process needs energy, and it also needs the right

17
00:00:54,822 --> 00:00:56,691
parts of the particles to meet.

18
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:00,140
So what might prevent a collision from producing products?

19
00:01:00,418 --> 00:01:04,226
The collision may have insufficient energy, or it may have

20
00:01:04,284 --> 00:01:07,140
an unsuitable spatial orientation.

21
00:01:07,535 --> 00:01:11,552
Those two conditions are the heart of collision theory.

22
00:01:11,726 --> 00:01:13,537
Here is the central idea.

23
00:01:13,746 --> 00:01:17,763
For a reaction to occur, reactant particles must collide.

24
00:01:18,007 --> 00:01:20,248
A collision alone is not enough.

25
00:01:20,457 --> 00:01:24,381
A successful collision must satisfy the energy requirement

26
00:01:24,590 --> 00:01:26,726
and the orientation requirement.

27
00:01:26,901 --> 00:01:30,593
If either requirement is not met, the collision is

28
00:01:30,709 --> 00:01:33,298
unsuccessful and products do not form.

29
00:01:33,750 --> 00:01:36,270
Let us take the energy requirement first.

30
00:01:36,479 --> 00:01:40,182
This is specification point A eleven point one

31
00:01:40,252 --> 00:01:41,169
point one.

32
00:01:41,506 --> 00:01:45,558
Activation energy is the minimum energy that colliding

33
00:01:45,639 --> 00:01:48,484
particles must have for a successful reaction.

34
00:01:49,099 --> 00:01:52,791
Particles need enough collision energy for the required bonds

35
00:01:52,849 --> 00:01:55,449
to break and for atoms to rearrange.

36
00:01:56,274 --> 00:02:00,163
New bonds can then form as products are produced.

37
00:02:00,871 --> 00:02:05,353
A collision with energy below the activation energy is unsuccessful.

38
00:02:05,469 --> 00:02:07,152
Watch this first animation.

39
00:02:07,326 --> 00:02:11,448
Two reactant particles approach and collide, but the collision

40
00:02:11,564 --> 00:02:14,536
energy is less than the activation energy.

41
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:17,531
The required bond breaking cannot occur.

42
00:02:17,647 --> 00:02:21,444
The particles separate without forming the specified products.

43
00:02:21,839 --> 00:02:24,230
Now watch a higher-energy collision.

44
00:02:24,509 --> 00:02:28,387
The particles collide with energy equal to or greater

45
00:02:28,445 --> 00:02:30,476
than the activation energy.

46
00:02:30,871 --> 00:02:34,761
There is now enough energy for the required bond changes.

47
00:02:34,819 --> 00:02:38,894
If the orientation is also correct, the collision can be successful,

48
00:02:38,941 --> 00:02:40,392
and products can form.

49
00:02:40,438 --> 00:02:43,503
This next point is easy to miss, and it is worth a

50
00:02:43,596 --> 00:02:46,418
mark in an examination question.

51
00:02:46,534 --> 00:02:50,063
Energy is necessary, but it is not sufficient.

52
00:02:50,586 --> 00:02:53,883
A collision can have enough energy and still fail.

53
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:58,283
The reacting parts of the particles must also meet in a suitable

54
00:02:58,341 --> 00:02:59,282
orientation.

55
00:02:59,897 --> 00:03:04,007
Collision theory therefore uses two tests: sufficient

56
00:03:04,077 --> 00:03:07,374
energy, and correct spatial orientation.

57
00:03:07,490 --> 00:03:11,298
That prevents the misconception that every high-energy collision must

58
00:03:11,379 --> 00:03:11,948
react.

59
00:03:12,122 --> 00:03:13,574
Use this decision rule.

60
00:03:13,690 --> 00:03:17,382
If collision energy is less than activation energy, the

61
00:03:17,463 --> 00:03:21,736
collision is unsuccessful because of insufficient energy.

62
00:03:22,351 --> 00:03:25,997
If collision energy is equal to or greater than activation

63
00:03:26,089 --> 00:03:29,700
energy, the energy requirement is satisfied, but you

64
00:03:29,781 --> 00:03:31,860
must still check orientation.

65
00:03:32,382 --> 00:03:36,190
A complete explanation refers to sufficient energy for

66
00:03:36,236 --> 00:03:39,963
the required bond breaking and rearrangement, and to

67
00:03:40,033 --> 00:03:41,763
the formation of new bonds.

68
00:03:42,378 --> 00:03:46,151
Now specification point A eleven point one point two:

69
00:03:46,395 --> 00:03:47,997
spatial orientation.

70
00:03:48,706 --> 00:03:52,142
Reacting particles have a three-dimensional arrangement.

71
00:03:52,537 --> 00:03:56,519
For some collisions, particular atoms or reactive regions

72
00:03:56,728 --> 00:03:57,993
must meet one another.

73
00:03:58,388 --> 00:04:02,034
If the particles approach in an unsuitable orientation,

74
00:04:02,370 --> 00:04:06,004
the necessary bond changes cannot occur, even if

75
00:04:06,062 --> 00:04:06,875
they touch.

76
00:04:07,850 --> 00:04:11,612
Correct spatial orientation is therefore a second

77
00:04:11,694 --> 00:04:14,376
requirement for a successful collision.

78
00:04:14,712 --> 00:04:16,767
Watch an incorrect orientation.

79
00:04:17,046 --> 00:04:20,680
The particles collide, but the required reactive regions

80
00:04:20,749 --> 00:04:24,383
do not meet appropriately, so there is no reaction.

81
00:04:24,778 --> 00:04:26,972
Now watch a correct orientation.

82
00:04:27,425 --> 00:04:30,351
The relevant parts of the reactants meet.

83
00:04:30,745 --> 00:04:34,391
The required bond rearrangement can occur if the collision

84
00:04:34,507 --> 00:04:36,481
energy is also sufficient.

85
00:04:37,002 --> 00:04:40,682
Orientation is about how particles meet, not simply whether they

86
00:04:40,764 --> 00:04:41,274
touch.

87
00:04:42,099 --> 00:04:43,596
Put the two tests together.

88
00:04:43,771 --> 00:04:47,567
Test one: is the collision energy at least the activation

89
00:04:47,648 --> 00:04:48,240
energy?

90
00:04:48,577 --> 00:04:52,257
Test two: are the particles aligned so the required

91
00:04:52,327 --> 00:04:54,452
atoms or regions can interact?

92
00:04:54,626 --> 00:04:57,319
Both yes: a successful collision is possible.

93
00:04:57,470 --> 00:05:00,187
Either no: the collision is unsuccessful.

94
00:05:00,431 --> 00:05:03,275
Let us apply this to numbers taken from the lesson.

95
00:05:03,449 --> 00:05:07,188
Reaction X has an activation energy of forty

96
00:05:07,269 --> 00:05:09,301
five kilojoules per mole.

97
00:05:09,475 --> 00:05:13,120
Collision A has thirty kilojoules per mole and the correct

98
00:05:13,167 --> 00:05:14,003
orientation.

99
00:05:14,281 --> 00:05:15,292
Is it successful?

100
00:05:15,466 --> 00:05:15,895
No.

101
00:05:16,139 --> 00:05:20,098
Thirty is below forty five, so the collision is unsuccessful

102
00:05:20,249 --> 00:05:23,941
because of insufficient energy, even though the orientation is

103
00:05:24,010 --> 00:05:24,475
correct.

104
00:05:24,811 --> 00:05:28,701
Collision B has fifty five kilojoules per mole, but the orientation

105
00:05:28,759 --> 00:05:29,548
is incorrect.

106
00:05:29,757 --> 00:05:33,530
The energy is sufficient, but the spatial orientation is wrong,

107
00:05:33,705 --> 00:05:36,154
so the collision is still unsuccessful.

108
00:05:36,491 --> 00:05:40,728
Collision C has fifty kilojoules per mole and the correct orientation.

109
00:05:41,065 --> 00:05:44,757
Energy exceeds activation energy and orientation

110
00:05:44,827 --> 00:05:48,101
is correct, so a successful collision is possible.

111
00:05:48,495 --> 00:05:49,691
Notice the pattern.

112
00:05:49,865 --> 00:05:53,523
Correct orientation cannot compensate for low energy, and

113
00:05:53,616 --> 00:05:57,122
high energy cannot compensate for the wrong orientation.

114
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:58,980
Both conditions are required.

115
00:05:59,131 --> 00:06:01,673
Here are four misconceptions to avoid.

116
00:06:01,789 --> 00:06:04,599
Particles do not react whenever they collide.

117
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:08,488
Energy and orientation conditions must be satisfied.

118
00:06:08,883 --> 00:06:12,343
Activation energy is not energy released by a reaction.

119
00:06:12,738 --> 00:06:16,325
It is the minimum energy required for a successful collision.

120
00:06:16,778 --> 00:06:21,189
Enough energy does not guarantee a reaction, because spatial orientation

121
00:06:21,340 --> 00:06:22,791
must also be correct.

122
00:06:22,907 --> 00:06:26,727
And correct orientation cannot make up for collision energy below

123
00:06:26,797 --> 00:06:28,236
the activation energy.

124
00:06:28,411 --> 00:06:31,545
Let us finish with an examination-style question.

125
00:06:31,719 --> 00:06:35,458
A reaction occurs when particles P and Q collide.

126
00:06:35,609 --> 00:06:39,510
The activation energy is sixty two kilojoules per mole.

127
00:06:39,684 --> 00:06:40,380
Part a.

128
00:06:40,473 --> 00:06:44,397
Collision one has fifty eight kilojoules per mole and the correct

129
00:06:44,479 --> 00:06:45,396
orientation.

130
00:06:45,791 --> 00:06:47,904
Explain why it is unsuccessful.

131
00:06:48,113 --> 00:06:49,575
That is worth two marks.

132
00:06:49,912 --> 00:06:53,929
Fifty eight is below sixty-two, so there is insufficient

133
00:06:54,022 --> 00:06:56,483
energy for the required bond changes.

134
00:06:57,098 --> 00:06:57,725
Part b.

135
00:06:57,841 --> 00:07:01,905
Collision two has seventy kilojoules per mole but incorrect

136
00:07:01,963 --> 00:07:02,776
orientation.

137
00:07:03,170 --> 00:07:05,492
Explain why it is unsuccessful.

138
00:07:05,829 --> 00:07:06,595
Two marks.

139
00:07:06,874 --> 00:07:10,937
The energy exceeds the activation energy, but the particles

140
00:07:11,019 --> 00:07:14,896
are not in the correct spatial orientation for the required

141
00:07:14,966 --> 00:07:15,779
interaction.

142
00:07:16,301 --> 00:07:17,102
Part c.

143
00:07:17,439 --> 00:07:21,073
State the conditions needed for a collision between P and Q

144
00:07:21,410 --> 00:07:22,582
to form products.

145
00:07:22,756 --> 00:07:23,476
Two marks.

146
00:07:23,755 --> 00:07:27,528
Collision energy must be at least the activation energy, and

147
00:07:27,587 --> 00:07:30,791
the particles must have the correct spatial orientation.

148
00:07:31,128 --> 00:07:31,847
Part d.

149
00:07:32,242 --> 00:07:36,190
Explain why collision theory predicts that many collisions do

150
00:07:36,282 --> 00:07:37,710
not produce a reaction.

151
00:07:38,163 --> 00:07:38,964
Two marks.

152
00:07:39,243 --> 00:07:43,411
Many collisions fail one or both requirements: insufficient

153
00:07:43,481 --> 00:07:46,256
energy, or incorrect orientation, or both.

154
00:07:46,372 --> 00:07:48,566
Before we finish, complete the model.

155
00:07:48,613 --> 00:07:50,505
Reactant particles must collide.

156
00:07:50,656 --> 00:07:54,743
The collision must have energy at least equal to the activation energy.

157
00:07:54,894 --> 00:07:58,573
That energy is needed so bonds can break and new bonds

158
00:07:58,631 --> 00:07:59,351
can form.

159
00:07:59,595 --> 00:08:03,066
The particles must also have the correct spatial orientation.

160
00:08:03,241 --> 00:08:07,165
Only collisions that satisfy both requirements are successful

161
00:08:07,246 --> 00:08:07,896
collisions.

162
00:08:08,070 --> 00:08:11,821
If you found this useful, please subscribe to J D Science,

163
00:08:11,972 --> 00:08:15,641
and visit j d science dot co dot u k for more

164
